\section{Displacement Distribution}
\label{sec:appendix_displacement}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig2_ee_displacement_distribution}
\caption{Displacement distribution across evaluation episodes. Pi0-family models (blue) cluster below 1\,$\mu$m, while Pi0.5 episodes (orange) exceed 200\,mm. The five-order-of-magnitude gap makes $\tau_d$ robust to any value in $[2, 200]$\,mm.}
\label{fig:displacement}
\end{figure}

\section{Displacement Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{sec:appendix_threshold}

To validate the robustness of our displacement threshold $\tau_d$, we evaluate the frozen rate across four orders of magnitude. Table~\ref{tab:threshold_sensitivity} shows that the fake safety pattern persists across all reasonable threshold choices: A1-LoRA exhibits near-total action collapse regardless of threshold, while Pi0.5+A1 maintains 0\% frozen rate even at the most conservative setting.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Frozen rate (\% of episodes with $d_\mathrm{max} < \tau_d$) across threshold values.}
\label{tab:threshold_sensitivity}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
$\tau_d$ & A1-LoRA (Pi0) & Pi0.5 Baseline & Pi0.5+A1 (SDF aux) \\
\midrule
0.001 mm (1\,$\mu$m) & 73.9 & 0.0 & 0.0 \\
0.1 mm  & 99.6 & 0.0 & 0.0 \\
1.0 mm  & 100.0 & 0.0 & 0.0 \\
10 mm   & 100.0 & 0.0 & 0.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The choice of $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm represents a conservative threshold: displacement below this value is physically negligible in manipulation tasks where typical action magnitudes are 1--10\,mm per step. Even at $\tau_d = 1$\,$\mu$m, 73.9\% of A1-LoRA safe episodes are classified as frozen, while all Pi0.5 episodes remain above threshold at every level tested.

Table~\ref{tab:dfcar_threshold} extends this analysis to dfCAR across fine-tuned Pi0.5 configurations. All eight model-seed combinations yield identical dfCAR for $\tau_d \in [2, 200]$\,mm. At $\tau_d = 300$\,mm, 0--2 episodes per seed fall below threshold (displacement 200--300\,mm), causing $\leq$0.5\,pp deviation. Our conclusions hold across the entire tested range.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{dfCAR (\%) across displacement thresholds for fine-tuned Pi0.5 configurations ($n = 200$ episodes per seed). Values are identical across $\tau_d \in [2, 200]$\,mm; at $\tau_d = 300$\,mm, at most 2 episodes are excluded.}
\label{tab:dfcar_threshold}
\vspace{2pt}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{8}{c}{$\tau_d$ (mm)} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-9}
Condition & 2 & 5 & 10 & 20 & 50 & 100 & 200 & 300 \\
\midrule
LoRA s0            & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 \\
LoRA s1            & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ s0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.1 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ s1 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 & 54.0 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ s2 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.5 & 52.3 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ s0  & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 & 57.0 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ s1  & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.5 & 57.1 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ s2  & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.5 & 54.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\section{Per-Task TSR Breakdown}\label{sec:appendix_tsr_pertask}

Table~\ref{tab:per_task_tsr} decomposes TSR by task for the avoidance source attribution experiments (Section~\ref{sec:avoidance_source}). All tasks retain meaningful success rates after fine-tuning, with Chocolate Pudding most resilient and Orange Juice most affected.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-task TSR (\%) at 30K training steps. LoRA-only reports seed~0 (rotation-fixed re-evaluation); SDF conditions report 3-seed means.}
\label{tab:per_task_tsr}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Task & Pi0.5 Base & LoRA-only\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ & SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ \\
 & (3 seeds) & (seed 0) & (3 seeds) & (3 seeds) \\
\midrule
Orange Juice      & 70.7 & 18.0 & 33.3 & 24.7 \\
Choco.\ Pudding   & 86.0 & 72.0 & 78.0 & 76.7 \\
Milk              & 66.0 & 32.0 & 35.3 & 48.7 \\
BBQ Sauce         & 76.0 & 62.0 & 69.3 & 65.3 \\
\midrule
Mean              & 74.7 & 46.0 & 54.0 & 53.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
{\footnotesize \textsuperscript{$\dagger$}Rotation-fixed re-evaluation; seed-1 evaluated with original rotation protocol, excluded to avoid mixing evaluation conditions.}
\end{table}

\section{Per-Task CAR Breakdown}\label{sec:appendix_pertask_car}

Table~\ref{tab:per_task_car} decomposes CAR by task for all fine-tuned conditions, supporting the claim that per-task ordering is preserved across SDF loss weights (\S\ref{sec:avoidance_source}). Chocolate Pudding is consistently highest and BBQ Sauce consistently lowest across all conditions, including $\lambda = 0.1$. The intermediate tasks (Orange Juice and Milk) exchange rank between seeds but remain bounded by the extremes. This invariance indicates that avoidance patterns reflect the spatial statistics of collision-free demonstrations rather than geometric supervision.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-task CAR (\%) at 30K steps. $\lambda{=}0.005$ and $\lambda{=}0.01$ report 3-seed means. Per-task ordering (CP highest, BBQ lowest) is preserved across all conditions.}
\label{tab:per_task_car}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Condition & OJ & CP & Milk & BBQ & Agg. \\
\midrule
LoRA s0 & 68 & 74 & 66 & 20 & 57.0 \\
LoRA s1 & 54 & 68 & 72 & 16 & 52.5 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ (3-seed) & 54.0 & 72.7 & 71.3 & 20.0 & 54.5 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ (3-seed) & 57.3 & 72.7 & 72.0 & 23.3 & 56.3 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.1$ s0 & 60 & 74 & 68 & 30 & 58.0 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.1$ s1 & 66 & 72 & 70 & 26 & 58.5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

For the $\lambda = 0.1$ condition, seed~1 records avg\_max\_obstacle\_displacement of 0.0553\,m (per-task: OJ\,=\,0.033, CP\,=\,0.055, Milk\,=\,0.041, BBQ\,=\,0.093), comparable to other fine-tuned conditions and consistent with active manipulation rather than frozen behavior.

\section{Safe Success Rate Analysis}\label{sec:appendix_ssr}

Table~\ref{tab:ssr} reports the Safe Success Rate (SSR\,=\,TSR\,$\cap$\,CAR), the fraction of episodes achieving both task completion and collision avoidance. The SSR/TSR ratio quantifies the proportion of successful episodes that are also collision-free, controlling for task-completion rate.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{SSR and SSR/TSR ratio across conditions. The ratio increase from 0.33 (baseline) to 0.51--0.58 (fine-tuned) indicates that CAR gains reflect learned safer execution, not reduced task exposure.}
\label{tab:ssr}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Condition & TSR (\%) & CAR (\%) & SSR (\%) & SSR/TSR \\
\midrule
Pi0.5 baseline & 74.7 & 26.8 & 25.0\textsuperscript{a} & 0.33 \\
LoRA-only (2 seeds) & 48.75 & 54.75 & 28.3 & 0.58 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ (3 seeds) & 54.0 & 54.5 & 27.8 & 0.51 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ (3 seeds) & 53.8 & 56.33 & 30.2 & 0.56 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.1$\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & 48.0 & 58.25 & 26.5 & 0.55 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
{\footnotesize \textsuperscript{a}Estimated; per-episode overlap unavailable for 3-seed rotation-corrected protocol. \textsuperscript{$\dagger$}SSR from seed~1 only; seed~0 per-episode data overwritten.}
\end{table}

\section{Per-Task Oracle Analysis}
\label{sec:appendix_pertask}
\label{sec:appendix_pertask_oracle}

Table~\ref{tab:oracle_pertask} shows per-task CAR for oracle refinement. On task~1 (\texttt{chocolate\_pudding}), where the EE is the primary collision body and the LoRA baseline achieves only 46.0\% CAR, Oracle-adaptive improves to 55.0\% and Oracle-feature to 47.5\%. On the remaining tasks, all oracle variants degrade: Oracle-adaptive reduces task~0 (\texttt{orange\_juice}) from 96.0\% to 82.5\% and task~2 (\texttt{milk}) from 98.0\% to 77.5\%. The SDF gradient pushes the EE away from the nearest surface, but the resulting joint-space displacement rotates intermediate arm links into obstacle volumes.

The feature-space oracle fires on nearly every timestep (241.7 refinements/episode vs.\ 14.2 for Cartesian variants) but with gradient magnitudes two orders of magnitude smaller (0.004--0.006 vs.\ 0.18--0.49), and CAR (76.9\%) shows no advantage despite 17$\times$ more steps. The A3 learned SDF never triggers refinement (0 steps/episode), as its outputs avoid the activation threshold.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-task CAR (\%) for oracle SDF refinement variants. EE-only refinement helps only on task~1 where the EE is the primary collision body; on other tasks it trades one collision site for another.}
\label{tab:oracle_pertask}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & Task 0 & Task 1 & Task 2 & Task 3 & Agg. \\
\midrule
LoRA baseline & 96.0 & 46.0 & 98.0 & 88.0 & 82.0 \\
\midrule
Oracle-adaptive & 82.5 & 55.0 & 77.5 & 85.0 & 75.0 \\
Oracle-fixed & 85.0 & 50.0 & 77.5 & 80.0 & 73.1 \\
Oracle-feature & 90.0 & 47.5 & 82.5 & 87.5 & 76.9 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\section{Null Distribution for SDF Loss Weight Analysis}
\label{sec:appendix_null}

We construct a null distribution by pooling the two LoRA-only seeds and three $\lambda = 0.005$ seeds into five per-seed CAR measurements: 57.0\% (LoRA s0, re-evaluated), 52.5\% (LoRA s1), 57.0\% ($\lambda{=}0.005$ s0), 54.0\% ($\lambda{=}0.005$ s1), 52.5\% ($\lambda{=}0.005$ s2), yielding mean 54.6\% and standard deviation 2.27\%. We treat these as exchangeable because SDF at $\lambda = 0.005$ produces no separation from LoRA-only. The $\lambda = 0.01$ mean of 56.33\% falls within $0.8\sigma$ of this null ($p > 0.1$), consistent with seed-to-seed variation rather than a treatment effect. The $\lambda = 0.1$ 30K mean of 58.25\% (2 seeds) yields $z = 2.27$ ($p \approx 0.023$), a marginal departure whose interpretation is limited by $n = 2$.

A Phase~2 ablation reinforces this conclusion. Setting $\lambda = 0$ during Phase~2 (steps 5K--30K), so that SDF supervision is active only during auxiliary head warmup (Phase~1, steps 0--5K), yields CAR of 57.5\% (single seed, s2). This value lies within the null range, indicating that SDF training produces no lasting behavioral effect on collision avoidance.

\section{Step-wise Checkpoint Analysis for \texorpdfstring{$\lambda = 0.1$}{lambda = 0.1}}
\label{sec:appendix_stepwise}

Table~\ref{tab:stepwise_lambda01} reports per-seed CAR at seven checkpoints for $\lambda = 0.1$. Seed-1 data is available at 16K, 20K, 22K, and 30K; 24K--28K report seed-0 only. The seed-0 curve shows no monotonic trend through 28K; the 30K checkpoint marks the first consistent dual-seed departure from the null band, though whether this reflects a genuine late-training effect or sampling variance at $n = 2$ remains open.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-seed CAR (\%) at training checkpoints for $\lambda = 0.1$ (200 episodes each). 24K--28K: seed-0 only. Dual-seed means remain within the null band (54.6\%\,$\pm$\,2.27\%) at all checkpoints except 30K, where both seeds depart marginally ($z = 2.27$, $p \approx 0.023$).}
\label{tab:stepwise_lambda01}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Checkpoint & Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Mean \\
\midrule
16K steps & 55.5 & 56.0 & 55.75 \\
20K steps & 55.0 & 56.0 & 55.5 \\
22K steps & 60.5 & 56.5 & 58.5 \\
24K steps & 52.5 & -- & -- \\
26K steps & 53.5 & -- & -- \\
28K steps & 54.5 & -- & -- \\
30K steps & 58.0 & 58.5 & 58.25 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Individual seeds exhibit checkpoint-to-checkpoint CAR fluctuations of up to 8\,pp (seed~0: 52.5--60.5\%), larger than the cross-seed standard deviation at any fixed checkpoint ($\leq$2.8\,pp). This is consistent with binomial sampling noise at $n = 50$ episodes per task ($\sigma \approx 7$\,pp per task for $p \approx 0.5$). The seed-averaged curve shows no monotonic trend, confirming that SDF loss weight does not produce a progressive training signal.


\section{Oracle SDF Refinement Details}
\label{sec:appendix_oracle}

All oracle variants replace the learned SDF decoder with ground-truth distance fields computed from the simulation geometry. Each is evaluated for 160 episodes (4 tasks $\times$ 40).

\paragraph{Oracle-adaptive.} At each timestep, if the ground-truth SDF at the end-effector position falls below a safety margin, the action is adjusted by a step in the negative SDF gradient direction. The step size $\alpha$ is scaled inversely with distance: smaller distances produce larger corrections. This mimics an adaptive potential field controller.

\paragraph{Oracle-fixed.} Identical to Oracle-adaptive except the step size is fixed at $\alpha = 0.005$ regardless of distance. This isolates the effect of step-size adaptation from the refinement geometry.

\paragraph{Oracle-feature.} Instead of modifying the action directly in Cartesian space, this variant applies the SDF gradient in the policy's latent feature space via backpropagation. The gradient of the ground-truth SDF value with respect to the intermediate features is computed and used to perturb the features before the action head decodes them. This tests whether geometric corrections are more effective when applied in the model's learned representation space. In practice, the feature-space variant fires on nearly every timestep (241.7 refinements per episode vs.\ 14.2 for Cartesian variants) but with gradient magnitudes two orders of magnitude smaller (0.004--0.006 vs.\ 0.18--0.49 in action space).


\section{Training Details}
\label{sec:appendix_training}

\paragraph{Architecture.}
We use LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} adapters injected into the PaliGemma vision encoder (rank 16, $\alpha$=16) and the action expert (rank 32, $\alpha$=32), following the configuration of OpenPi. The SDF decoder is a 4-layer MLP (hidden dimension 256) with skip connections, taking pooled suffix features (1024-d) and query points (3-d) as input. The end-effector extractor is a 2-layer MLP (1024 $\to$ 256 $\to$ 3).

\paragraph{Training schedule.}
Training proceeds in two phases:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Phase 1} (steps 0--5000): Backbone and LoRA parameters are frozen. Only the SDF decoder and EE extractor are trained, allowing them to learn meaningful representations before the policy adapts.
    \item \textbf{Phase 2} (steps 5000--30000): LoRA parameters are unfrozen with a 100-step gradient warmup ramp. All auxiliary heads continue training jointly with the policy.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Hyperparameters.}
\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Training hyperparameters.}
\label{tab:hyperparams}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Parameter & Value \\
\midrule
Base model & Pi0.5 (OpenPi, $\sim$3.5B parameters) \\
Optimizer & AdamW \\
Learning rate & $2.5 \times 10^{-5}$ (cosine decay to $\mathrm{lr}/10$) \\
Warmup steps & 1000 \\
Batch size & 32 \\
Total steps & 30000 \\
$\lambda_\mathrm{sdf}$ & \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\} \\
$\lambda_\mathrm{ee}$ & 0.01 \\
SDF query points per step & 64 \\
SDF clamp $\delta$ & 0.05 m \\
Eikonal weight & 0.1 \\
LoRA rank (vision) & 16 \\
LoRA rank (action) & 32 \\
Phase 1 duration & 5000 steps \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Evaluation protocol.}
Each episode runs for at most 300 timesteps. The policy generates a 50-step action trajectory via flow matching and executes the first 5 steps before replanning. An episode terminates early upon task completion. Collision detection uses MuJoCo's built-in contact detection: any contact between the robot and a non-target object registers as a collision. For Pi0.5 evaluations, camera images are rotated 180$^\circ$ to match the pre-training viewpoint convention; this correction was verified to restore TSR from ${\sim}$5\% to ${\sim}$52\% while leaving CAR comparable. Oracle variants use 40 episodes per task (160 total) with a single seed; all other configurations use 50 episodes per task (200 total).

\paragraph{Data.}
We use the SafeLIBERO object\_pudding suite with pre-computed SDF ground truth. SDF values are computed offline from the obstacle mesh using a dense $64^3$ voxel grid. During training, 64 query points are randomly sampled per step from this grid.

\paragraph{Computational resources.}
All training and evaluation experiments are conducted on NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (96\,GB). Phase~1 (5K steps) takes approximately 6 hours on a single GPU; Phase~2 (25K steps) takes approximately 32 hours. Evaluation of 200 episodes requires approximately 4 hours. Across 100+ experiments (training runs, evaluations, oracle variants, and ablations), the total computational budget is approximately 700 GPU-hours.

